1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,632
The college majors with the lowest unemployment rate right now aren't in tech.

2
00:00:04,632 --> 00:00:05,820
They're in teaching.

3
00:00:05,820 --> 00:00:08,789
Special education grads: zero point seven percent.

4
00:00:08,909 --> 00:00:11,533
So which majors are actually safe from AI?

5
00:00:11,883 --> 00:00:13,253
No major is AI-proof.

6
00:00:13,253 --> 00:00:15,274
Nobody's data can promise that.

7
00:00:15,274 --> 00:00:18,992
What the New York Fed data can show is narrower: in 2024,

8
00:00:18,992 --> 00:00:23,361
which majors' new grads were finding work, and what they were paid.

9
00:00:23,711 --> 00:00:26,014
Start with the lowest unemployment.

10
00:00:26,014 --> 00:00:28,252
Education majors dominate the top.

11
00:00:28,252 --> 00:00:31,279
Special education at zero point seven percent,

12
00:00:31,279 --> 00:00:33,780
elementary education at one point two.

13
00:00:33,900 --> 00:00:35,137
But look at the pay.

14
00:00:35,137 --> 00:00:39,094
Special education grads start around forty-six thousand dollars.

15
00:00:39,094 --> 00:00:41,135
Elementary education, forty-five.

16
00:00:41,135 --> 00:00:45,463
The typical starting salary across all majors is fifty-eight thousand.

17
00:00:45,583 --> 00:00:46,710
Safe by one measure.

18
00:00:46,710 --> 00:00:48,569
Among the lowest paid by another.

19
00:00:48,919 --> 00:00:52,077
The more useful list is the majors that do well on both.

20
00:00:52,077 --> 00:00:56,194
Below-average unemployment, and starting pay of seventy thousand or more.

21
00:00:56,314 --> 00:01:00,456
Nursing: two point one percent unemployed, seventy thousand to start.

22
00:01:00,456 --> 00:01:04,478
And only about thirteen percent of nursing grads are underemployed,

23
00:01:04,478 --> 00:01:06,639
against thirty-nine percent overall.

24
00:01:06,759 --> 00:01:10,731
Civil engineering: two point three percent, seventy-five thousand.

25
00:01:10,731 --> 00:01:14,763
Aerospace engineering: two point two percent, eighty-five thousand.

26
00:01:14,763 --> 00:01:18,855
Construction services: two point two percent, seventy-five thousand.

27
00:01:18,975 --> 00:01:22,474
Electrical engineering, finance, and economics make the list too.

28
00:01:22,594 --> 00:01:25,237
Compare computer science: higher pay to start,

29
00:01:25,237 --> 00:01:28,397
at eighty-seven thousand, but seven percent unemployed.

30
00:01:28,747 --> 00:01:32,140
Many of the sweet-spot majors lead to work tied to a place,

31
00:01:32,140 --> 00:01:36,109
a license, or a patient: hospitals, job sites, aircraft, power grids.

32
00:01:36,109 --> 00:01:39,157
That's our reading, not something the table measures.

33
00:01:39,277 --> 00:01:42,110
It lines up with two findings from our first video.

34
00:01:42,110 --> 00:01:44,665
The New York Fed found the gap for young grads

35
00:01:44,665 --> 00:01:46,943
opened in jobs that can be done remotely,

36
00:01:46,943 --> 00:01:48,387
like software engineering.

37
00:01:48,507 --> 00:01:53,371
And Stanford payroll data shows employment in the jobs most exposed

38
00:01:53,371 --> 00:01:57,655
to AI fell about eleven percent from late 2022 to mid-2026,

39
00:01:57,655 --> 00:02:01,285
while in the least exposed jobs it rose about ten.

40
00:02:01,405 --> 00:02:02,726
Patterns, not proof.

41
00:02:02,726 --> 00:02:07,549
And these figures are one year, 2024, with small samples for some majors.

42
00:02:07,899 --> 00:02:09,683
So, which majors are AI-proof?

43
00:02:09,683 --> 00:02:10,574
None, honestly.

44
00:02:10,574 --> 00:02:13,250
But some majors are doing well on both counts

45
00:02:13,250 --> 00:02:15,806
that matter for a first job: getting hired,

46
00:02:15,806 --> 00:02:16,817
and getting paid.

47
00:02:16,937 --> 00:02:19,885
A better question than "is it AI-proof?" is this:

48
00:02:19,885 --> 00:02:22,772
does this major lead to work that AI helps with,

49
00:02:22,772 --> 00:02:24,036
or work that AI does?

50
00:02:24,036 --> 00:02:25,299
And what does it pay?
